.Dd September 27, 2026
.Dt TIMERFD 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm timerfd_create ,
.Nm timerfd_settime ,
.Nm timerfd_gettime
.Nd timers read through a descriptor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/timerfd.h
.Ft int
.Fn timerfd_create "int clockid" "int flags"
.Ft int
.Fn timerfd_settime "int fd" "int flags" "const struct itimerspec *new" "struct itimerspec *old"
.Ft int
.Fn timerfd_gettime "int fd" "struct itimerspec *cur"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn timerfd_create ,
number 322, makes a timer on the clock
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME ,
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC
or
.Dv CLOCK_BOOTTIME ,
disarmed, and returns a descriptor for it;
.Fa flags
may be
.Dv TFD_CLOEXEC
and
.Dv TFD_NONBLOCK .
.Pp
.Fn timerfd_settime ,
number 325, arms the timer to expire first after
.Fa new Ns -> Ns Fa it_value ,
or at that time on its clock with
.Dv TFD_TIMER_ABSTIME ,
and then every
.Fa it_interval ,
or disarms it when
.Fa it_value
is zero; the setting it replaces goes to
.Fa old
when that is not NULL.
An absolute time already past expires at the next tick.
.Dv TFD_TIMER_CANCEL_ON_SET
is accepted and does nothing: a change of the real-time clock does not
move an armed timer.
.Fn timerfd_gettime ,
number 326, reports the time to the next expiration and the interval,
zeros for a disarmed timer.
The 64-bit time forms are numbers 411 and 410.
.Pp
A
.Xr read 2
of eight bytes returns the number of expirations since the last read or
setting, and waits while there are none, or fails with
.Er EAGAIN
for a nonblocking descriptor.
.Xr poll 2
and
.Xr epoll 2
report the descriptor readable while there are expirations to read.
.Pa /proc/ Ns Ar pid Ns Pa /fd
names it
.Dq anon_inode:[timerfd] .
.Pp
The kernel keeps time in 10 ms ticks: an expiration and an interval are
rounded up to whole ticks, and the tick in progress is not counted, so a
timer never expires early.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn timerfd_create
returns the descriptor, and the others 0; each returns -1 with
.Va errno
set on failure.
.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width EMFILE
.It Er EBADF
.Fa fd
is not an open descriptor.
.It Er EFAULT
A
.Vt struct itimerspec
is not in the caller's memory.
.It Er EINVAL
The clock or a flag is not one of those above;
.Fa fd
is not a timer; a nanosecond field is outside 0 to 999999999, or a time
is negative; or a read was shorter than eight bytes.
.It Er EMFILE
The process has no free descriptor.
.It Er EPERM
The clock is
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME_ALARM
or
.Dv CLOCK_BOOTTIME_ALARM ,
which need a privilege this kernel does not grant.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr clock_gettime 2 ,
.Xr epoll 2 ,
.Xr poll 2
